Your equipment combinations are quite standard and very well choices,
though I always prefer to have some ESP (helm in the first case, amulet
or helm in the second case). But what I actually wear in my games
depends not only on what I've found but also what will give me the best
gain given my played character and concrete situation in the game.

> The warning intrinsic
> (or ring, though I wouldn't waste a ring slot on this personally) and
> the detect monsters spell can provide substantially equivalent
> information, and work even for monsters that don't have brains.

I don't use that spell since (AFAIK) the perma-monster-display effect
one will get only with a trained skill. In case I don't have ESP and/or
warning I prefer the simple blindfold over the non-blessed potion or
unskilled spell. If I happen to have some blessed potions of monster
detection I save them usually for the Sanctum and/or for Astral.

> Regardless, I recognize that my equipment preferences are partly due
> to my preferred playstyle: not wishing for quest artifacts, and
> spellcasting even for fighter roles. If that's "playing with one hand
> tied behind my back", so be it; I did state that this is my playstyle
> and preference. I would be curious to see how many serial ascenders
> rely on the amulet of ESP as part of their kit, however...

ESP is an extraordinary good life saver in the early and mid game,
later it helps to play a speedy game. On the final home-run it's of
less use because it can't warn you about Rodney's summoned monsters.
But on Astral it's again of good use to locate the Riders and thus
be able to adjust your moves accordingly. One or two blessed potion
of monster detection are an appropriate substitute here, but one
potion weighs the same as an amulet, I think, so again I prefer the
item with the permanent effect.

WRT "kit"; for me it's not in the same category as MR and therefore
it's no strict requirement for an ascension kit.

